\section{Appendix A: Extraction and metric setup}
\begin{outline}
\item [+] Validation $r$ (probe accuracy) saturates ($\sim$0.99) across bandwidths, statement counts, and RFM iterations $\Rightarrow$ any selection \emph{by} val-$r$ is third-decimal noise; it is also a poor steerability predictor. Boxplots: \texttt{box\_rsq.png}.
\item [+] $\lambda_1$/trace at bandwidth $L{=}1$ discriminates across concepts; $L{=}10$ collapses it toward 1 (rank-1 AGOP by construction). \texttt{box\_l1trace.png}, \texttt{box\_l1l2.png}.
\item [+] The released $\lambda_1$/trace (read at the best-$r$ iteration) is unstable --- it samples a value climbing $0.0002 \to 0.17 \to 0.46$ across iterations at a noise-chosen point; reading at convergence cuts the statement-count swing 3--4$\times$. Both vector and metrics read at a fixed (converged) iteration.
\item [+] Saved per-layer extraction stats: top-3 eigenvalues (\texttt{evals}), natural concept magnitude (\texttt{magn} = raw projection gap), clamp targets (\texttt{tpos}, \texttt{tneg}), mean residual norm (\texttt{hnorm}) --- in the rfmstats pickles.
\item [?] Per-concept ``best layer'' by argmax is unstable: val-$r$ best is noise-driven; $\lambda_1$/trace and $\lambda_1/\lambda_2$ are bimodal with a degenerate spike at the first block (exclude early blocks). \texttt{besthist\_*.png}.
\item [?] Hyper-parameter sweep factors (probe set of 24 concepts, one per supersense): bandwidth $\times$ normalization $\times$ contrastive construction (now incl.\ within-category negatives) $\times$ iteration readout; criteria: stability, discrimination, semantic signal (Spearman vs.\ concreteness/polysemy), steering non-degradation. Artifacts: \texttt{hpsweep\_phase2\_*}.
\end{outline}

\section{Appendix B: Predicting steerability (pilot 3 classes + 600-concept main run)}
\begin{outline}
\item [+] Best single predictor of steering success is $\lambda_1$/trace at a mid layer (depth~9, grouped CV-AUC $\approx$0.86); $\lambda_1/\lambda_2$ similar; val-$r$ erratic (saturated). Logistic regressions in \texttt{nb\_regxp1.ipynb}; \texttt{L9\_metrics\_by\_steerability.png}.
\item [--] Multivariate regression on \emph{all} layers' metrics is uninformative --- severe multicollinearity (the residual stream is cumulative). Univariate per-layer CV-AUC is the clean read.
\item [+] \textbf{Sign flips with depth}: $\lambda_1$/trace predicts steerability \emph{positively} early (depth~9) but \emph{negatively} late (depth~29) --- late-layer concentration is lexical collapse, not concept concentration. \texttt{l1l2\_depth29\_dist.png}.
\item [?] Concept class is a confounder/mediator: most steerability variance is between-class; within-class signal is weak. Do not ``control for class'' if class is on the causal path (concreteness $\to$ signature coherence $\to$ steerability).
\item [?] \textbf{The inversion}: places have the \emph{highest} band-averaged $\lambda_1$/trace yet fail single-layer steering, while moods (lowest) succeed --- the metric certifies \emph{coherence} of the extracted blend, not its \emph{identity}. Predictor validity is contrast-dependent (main text, Pilot Finding~2).
\item [?] Participation ratio $\approx$2--3 for all concepts at all layers --- the discriminative subspace at one layer is effectively rank 2--3, bounding what top-$k$ can recover. \texttt{PR\_depth9\_hist.png}.
\item [+] Error analysis at depth~9: misclassifications symmetric, in a narrow overlap band; characterizes the $\sim$0.86 ceiling.
\item [+] \textbf{[NEW jul6] Main-run robustness: spectral collinearity.} The three spectral metrics are strongly inter-correlated at $n{=}600$ (PR--$\lambda_1$/trace $r{=}-.85$, $\lambda_1$/trace--$\lambda_1/\lambda_2$ $r{=}.82$; VIF up to 12), so joint coefficients are unstable. One-spectral-at-a-time ladder: PR alone $\beta{=}-0.27$ ($p{=}.023$, AIC 588.0); $\lambda_1/\lambda_2$ alone marginal ($p{=}.086$); $\lambda_1$/trace alone \emph{null and sign-flipped} ($\beta{=}+0.02$, $p{=}.89$) --- its large full-model coefficient is a collinearity artifact. PR is the reliable spectral predictor. \texttt{nb\_analysis600.ipynb}.
\item [+] \textbf{[NEW jul6] Main-run robustness: lexical predictors are clean.} Concreteness--Zipf $r{=}.09$; all lexical VIF $<2.7$; the conc($-$)/zipf($+$) coefficients are stable across all specifications (with/without spectral terms, fixed vs.\ mixed effects).
\item [+] \textbf{[NEW jul6] Main-run robustness: mixed-effects check.} Bayesian mixed logistic with a supersense random intercept reproduces the fixed-effects signs and magnitudes (PR $z{=}-6.6$, conc $z{=}-4.3$, zipf $z{=}+3.4$); supersense \emph{fixed} dummies fail AIC against the continuous predictors (626.7 supersense-only vs.\ 576.7 lexical+spectral; adding dummies to the best model worsens it to 597.9).
\item [+] \textbf{[NEW jul6] Outcome-robustness under the graded judge (V8).} Re-running the regressions with the graded score as outcome: concreteness ($-$) and spectral concentration (PR $-$, largest weight; spectral block $\Delta$AIC $\approx$ 16) survive the outcome switch; the Zipf effect shrinks to marginal --- part of the binary-judge frequency effect is plausibly \emph{nameability} (frequent words surface verbatim and earn binary credit; V8 also credits semantic-field movement). Caveat: under V8, PR is significant only jointly with $\lambda_1$/trace, not solo --- the conservative claim is ``spectral shape adds predictive power, PR carrying most of it.'' Main text keeps the binary V1$\cup$V6 outcome (closest to the RFM benchmark's evaluation model); V8 is the secondary analysis. \texttt{nb\_analysis600.ipynb}.
\item [?] \textbf{[NEW jul6] Note the pilot--main-run tension on $\lambda_1$/trace:} mid-layer $\lambda_1$/trace was the best pilot predictor (CV-AUC $\approx$0.86, 3 classes) but is null at $n{=}600$ under median-over-layers aggregation and all-layer steering. Candidate explanations: layer-specific vs.\ aggregated readout; 3-class between-class variance vs.\ 24-supersense within-class; contrast dependence (Pilot Finding~2). Worth one targeted analysis (per-layer readout on the 600) before the camera-ready. \textbf{[NEW jul10] RESOLVED:} the null was a v2b artifact --- under dual frames the top eigenvector is captured by the within-positive frame axis for weak concepts (Appendix~F, axis-alignment diagnostic); under the constant-prefix v4 frame $\lambda_1$/trace is significant alone ($+0.55^{**}$) with the pilot's sign.
\item [+] Figures for this appendix: \texttt{L9\_metrics\_by\_steerability} (Fig.~\ref{fig:l9metrics}); V8 validity scatter (Fig.~\ref{fig:v8scatter}).
\item [+] \textbf{[NEW jul10] v4-instrument regressions (now the main numbers; Table 2 in main text).} Llama: all four lexical predictors significant (conc $-0.20^{*}$, zipf $+0.28^{*}$, AoA $-0.33^{**}$, polysemy $-0.30^{**}$); each spectral metric alone beats the entire lexical block (AICs 775--787 vs.\ 799; null 816); best model lex$+$PR (763.6, PR $-0.62^{**}$). Gemma replicates all lexical signs (AoA $-0.49^{**}$ strongest) and PR at its own informative depth (60--70\%: $-0.42^{**}$ alone, $-0.39^{**}$ jointly, AIC 750.9) while null at Llama's 40\% convention --- same law, model-specific depth. Univariate honesty note: polysemy and concreteness do \emph{not} separate in raw boxplots (MW $p{>}.6$; effects are conditional); zipf/AoA/PR separate cleanly in both models (\texttt{plots/covariate\_boxplots\_fv4.png}). Spectral collinearity under v4 is extreme (PR--frac1 $r{\le}-.94$): one-at-a-time only.
\end{outline}

